1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:07,360
Uh, imagine you're sitting in your ministry, and your minister just approved $50 million for a massive new digital education program.

2
00:00:07,660 --> 00:00:08,860
Right, which is a huge win.

3
00:00:09,120 --> 00:00:09,860
Yeah. Exactly.

4
00:00:10,460 --> 00:00:19,000
But, uh, the ink is barely dry on the budget, and before a single line of code is written, there is this massive question you have to ask, and it has nothing to do with servers or tech.

5
00:00:19,580 --> 00:00:21,220
Oh, it's entirely about ownership.

6
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:27,280
Right. Is this new system gonna be shared by every department, or is your program keeping it entirely to itself?

7
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:32,820
So welcome to today's deep dive into digital public infrastructure, or DPI.

8
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:39,420
Our mission today is to figure out how governments can build these massive systems without paying for the exact same foundation twice.

9
00:00:39,940 --> 00:00:44,880
Because if you get that ownership question wrong, your ministry ends up with fragmented siloed data.

10
00:00:45,160 --> 00:00:50,720
Yeah, and you end up getting locked into one supplier, which makes everything incredibly costly to change down the line.

11
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,960
Exactly. You just end up re-keying the same data over and over.

12
00:00:54,620 --> 00:01:01,080
So to stop that waste, we really have to look at the underlying reference architecture of how modern government tech is stacked.

13
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:06,740
So let's visualize that stack. In the reference architecture, it's mapped out into three distinct layers.

14
00:01:06,860 --> 00:01:08,780
Uh, think of it kind of like building a city.

15
00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:11,900
At the very bottom, you have the governance foundations.

16
00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:13,500
Right, the laws.

17
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:20,760
Yeah, exactly. It's not tech at all. It's the leadership, the data protection policies, uh, the legal frameworks that make the whole system trustworthy.

18
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:25,780
And once those laws are in place, you move up to the middle layer, which is the core DPI.

19
00:01:26,660 --> 00:01:31,120
If we stick to your city metaphor, this is basically the municipal water and power grid.

20
00:01:31,580 --> 00:01:37,420
So we're talking about things like a national digital identity system, uh, digital payment rails, and data sharing.

21
00:01:37,960 --> 00:01:38,420
Precisely.

22
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:41,880
You build that grid once, and it serves the entire country.

23
00:01:42,540 --> 00:01:45,400
Which brings us to the top layer, uh, the sector applications.

24
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:51,040
So this is where digital education, digital health, these are the schools and hospitals built inside the city.

25
00:01:51,380 --> 00:01:51,460
Yeah.

26
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:55,760
But, I mean, here is a question a director general would definitely ask.

27
00:01:56,040 --> 00:02:00,920
If a school system sits on top of the core, doesn't that mean it's actually part of the core grid?

28
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:03,360
That is where the duplication happens.

29
00:02:03,980 --> 00:02:06,960
The education program uses the core, but it doesn't build it.

30
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:12,680
Your sector systems stand firmly on top of the core DPI, but they are entirely separate from it.

31
00:02:13,180 --> 00:02:19,580
Oh, so the government funds the core once, and then education just uses its own budget for its specific tools.

32
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:20,300
Exactly.

33
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:25,440
So let's apply that to data registries because obviously every ministry collects data.

34
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,160
Right. And the reference architecture is very clear on how registries fit into this.

35
00:02:31,060 --> 00:02:32,620
There are three main facts you need to know.

36
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:33,280
Uh,

37
00:02:34,440 --> 00:02:35,120
I'll just number them.

38
00:02:35,560 --> 00:02:35,880
Go for it.

39
00:02:36,360 --> 00:02:36,800
Number one,

40
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:40,780
digital ID and payments are basic national infrastructure.

41
00:02:41,360 --> 00:02:46,720
Okay. Number two, an education register is one of the main state registries that a country should have.

42
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:48,280
And number three,

43
00:02:48,920 --> 00:02:52,000
a state registry needs two specific building blocks to work:

44
00:02:53,080 --> 00:02:54,000
a registration block

45
00:02:54,740 --> 00:02:56,180
and a digital registry block.

46
00:02:57,020 --> 00:02:58,480
Okay, wait, let me push back on that a bit.

47
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:06,300
If education needs an identity system to track students, why shouldn't they just build their own functional identity system?

48
00:03:06,720 --> 00:03:12,040
But- Like, if they have the budget for an education register, why plug into the national grid at all?

49
00:03:12,560 --> 00:03:15,220
Because if they build it themselves, it's completely duplicated effort.

50
00:03:16,080 --> 00:03:19,220
The reference architecture dictates a single foundational identity.

51
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:22,080
That core identity simply proves who you are.

52
00:03:22,820 --> 00:03:23,700
I am Jane Doe.

53
00:03:24,360 --> 00:03:28,320
Right. A functional identity proves a role, like, "I am a fourth grade student."

54
00:03:29,160 --> 00:03:34,900
If the foundational ID already exists, your ministry building a brand-new system to prove her identity again is...

55
00:03:35,260 --> 00:03:36,280
Well, it's totally redundant.

56
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:42,500
So they just need to verify against the national ID and attach the student data to their own register.

57
00:03:42,920 --> 00:03:46,240
Exactly. Education does not build an identity system.

58
00:03:46,480 --> 00:03:48,420
Okay, let's look at how this plays out in practice.

59
00:03:48,620 --> 00:03:50,620
Uh, let's take a fictional country, Progressa.

60
00:03:50,740 --> 00:03:55,420
Right, Progressa. When Progressa rolls out a new digital initiative, they split the architecture.

61
00:03:55,840 --> 00:03:58,740
At the governance layer, they enforce their data protection laws.

62
00:03:59,320 --> 00:04:05,000
At the core layer, they fund a unified national identity and the payments building block.

63
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:10,080
Which means the education sector in Progressa only has to fund their specific learner register.

64
00:04:10,580 --> 00:04:13,320
They aren't spending education dollars on identity verification.

65
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:14,700
Because it's already there.

66
00:04:14,900 --> 00:04:19,140
Right. Identity, payments, and data exchange are the core serving every sector.

67
00:04:19,820 --> 00:04:23,740
Sector-specific systems, like a learner register, simply plug into them.

68
00:04:24,260 --> 00:04:28,500
Fund each layer exactly as what it is, and you avoid duplicating your infrastructure.

